{"caseId": "sci-026", "material": {"title": "Unveiling IC-STAR: Full-Flow Autonomy from Digital to Analog", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-29T10:00:06+00:00", "sourceName": "IEEE Spectrum · AI", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Learn how engineers can shift from manually managing tools and handoffs to defining objectives and supervising AI-driven execution across the silicon development lifecycle. Key Takeaways Explore four critical technologies enabling silicon design autonomy Understand how autonomous AI accelerates complex engineering workflows See real-world impact across semiconductor design and verification Gain practical insights from Ambiq’s deployment of autonomous AI in production Register now for this free webinar!"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "reject"}}
{"caseId": "sci-028", "material": {"title": "Generative AI Gives Spacecraft the Autonomy Engineers Once Feared", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-28T11:00:05+00:00", "sourceName": "IEEE Spectrum · AI", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Space was always supposed to be the final frontier of human exploration. It’s shaping up to be the final frontier for artificial intelligence too. Last December, NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory used Anthropic’s Claude models to help plan two Mars drives for the Perseverance rover , with human planners checking and adjusting the route before upload. In May, NASA and IBM put a compressed AI model on the International Space Station and a satellite to identify things like floods and clouds from orbit, the first model of its kind demonstrated in space. And in July, astronauts on the ISS tested a l"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-031", "material": {"title": "How AI does, and does not, change the way I do math", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-30T00:19:10+00:00", "sourceName": "Terence Tao", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "[This is a guest post by Rachel Webb . This blog post was initially written in a different file format and converted using AI. — T.] The way I do math experienced a great upheaval once before, halfway through my PhD. I was in a seminar with a group of graduate students and faculty developing new discipline-specific writing courses. The facilitator asked the question, What does it mean to write well in your discipline? Then, as now, I use writing as a proxy for doing. As a mid-career grad student, I had a quick answer to this question: good mathematical writing, hence good mathematics, is clear"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "holdout", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-032", "material": {"title": "Joint Statement about Mathathon", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-28T18:33:48+00:00", "sourceName": "Terence Tao", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "[This is a guest post by a coalition of Caltech mathematicians: the original organizers of Mathathon (Alvan Arulandu, Andrea Li, Avni Garg, Brian Zhao, Caiman Moreno-Earle, Sathvik Redrouthu), two coauthors of the Open Letter about the Mathathon (Dylan King, Tasmin Chu), and other members of the Caltech community (Mayla Ward, Merrick Hua, Robert Joseph George, Sergei Gukov, Shaowu Zhang, Tony Yue Yu, Vihaan Dheer). This blog post was initially written in a different file format and converted using AI. It is also crossposted at Proofs and Prompts — T.] Mathathon is centered around one question:"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-039", "material": {"title": "FLT: Anthropic has beaten me to it", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-04T18:30:42+00:00", "sourceName": "Kevin Buzzard · Xena", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "I guess technically it was revealed to the world by a coffee shop in Islington on Insta, but an hour later it was officially announced by Anthropic : one of their internal models, using the prove2.me platform, has formalized a complete proof of Fermat’s Last Theorem (FLT) in Lean. This is the final theorem to be formalized in Freek Wiedijk’s famous list of 100 formalization challenges and thus wraps up this 20-year-old benchmark. Congratulations to Anthropic! Mathematical details The proof is not the modern proof which I have been formalizing myself following ideas of Khare, Taylor etc, but th"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-040", "material": {"title": "The Annals Challenge", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-08-13T18:04:19+00:00", "sourceName": "Kevin Buzzard · Xena", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "My team of post-docs funded by this Renaissance Philanthropy grant has constructed a dataset of 50 Lean statements , corresponding to 50 important recent mathematical theorems! The 50 theorems were all published in the Annals of Mathematics (a prestigious mathematical journal) in the 2020s. We build on mathlib , Lean’s mathematics library; my team has built many new definitions and got them merged into mathlib as part of this project. As well as the dataset, we would like to issue two challenges related to it. A challenge for humans — state more theorems We have formalized the statements of ar"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-041", "material": {"title": "Human mathematicians are being outcounterexampled", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-07-20T09:53:22+00:00", "sourceName": "Kevin Buzzard · Xena", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "It’s been an interesting few weeks for counterexamples. This post is basically my perspective of what has been going on in the world of formalization, AI tools and, in particular, counterexamples. Unit distance Two months ago today (20th May 2026), ChatGPT disproved Erdős’ Unit Distance conjecture in discrete geometry. This is now old news but I had to start somewhere. The announcement was accompanied with testimonies by human mathematicians, many of whom I knew and a few of whom I trusted, saying that they believed the argument (they had been given early access to it and had checked it). The "}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "holdout", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-043", "material": {"title": "Import AI 474: Platonic mindspace; TPUs in space; Zhipu starts an outer RSI loop", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-28T12:32:03+00:00", "sourceName": "Import AI", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Welcome to Import AI, a newsletter about AI research. Import AI runs on arXiv, cappuccinos, and feedback from readers. If you’d like to support this, please subscribe. Subscribe now Are minds patterns from a Platonic space, with bodies and machines as their interfaces, Michael Levin asks: …A mind-bending paper asking us to reconsider basic assumptions about philosophy and biology… Could the complexity in the world around us be better explained by there being a platonic zone of patterns of arbitrary complexity which seek to embed themselves in the world? That’s the big question at the heart of "}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "reject"}}
{"caseId": "sci-049", "material": {"title": "How to Terraform Mars — Erika DeBenedictis of Pioneer Labs", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-22T15:47:45+00:00", "sourceName": "Asimov Press", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Pioneer Labs, a nonprofit research group in California, revealed today that they’ve engineered a “first” microbe that is able to live on Mars, using only nutrients found on the planet, and create building materials. They are saying this is the first ‘pioneer’ species needed to terraform the planet. During my interview with CEO Erika DeBenedictis, I asked about the myriad bottlenecks to growing life on Mars. The planet has tons of water, for example, but only as ice. (So much water, in fact, that if you melted all of it, it would fill an ocean 100 feet deep across the entire planet!) Similarly,"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "reject"}}
{"caseId": "sci-051", "material": {"title": "AI is getting cheaper faster than any other transformative technology", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-28T15:33:44+00:00", "sourceName": "Epoch AI", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "This is a summary of a longer report on our website. Would you be surprised if the sticker price on a new car fell from $50,000 to $296 in two years? Because that’s how fast AI is getting cheaper. Over the past five years, the price of thought — the cost for an AI to hit a particular benchmark score — has fallen 13× per year. It seems to be a faster pace than any other transformative technology has matched: it’s four times faster than DNA sequencing, six times faster than compute, 18 times faster than lithium batteries, and (in the century up to 1973) 54 times faster than electricity. (OK, we "}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T1", "firstParty": true, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "reject"}}
{"caseId": "sci-052", "material": {"title": "How far behind Nvidia is Huawei?", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-24T16:04:51+00:00", "sourceName": "Epoch AI", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "This is a summary of a more detailed report , available on our website. The US-China AI competition spans everything from models and data centers to chips and the manufacturing equipment used to make them. Chips and manufacturing equipment form the foundation of that stack and determine how much compute each side can field. This is why semiconductor export controls have been a centerpiece of US AI policy, and why so much depends on how China’s homegrown chip industry stacks up against America’s. Huawei, China’s leading AI chip designer, laid out its chip roadmap last year and showcased an acce"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T1", "firstParty": true, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "holdout", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "reject"}}
{"caseId": "sci-054", "material": {"title": "An update on AI’s most important number", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-08-27T21:46:41+00:00", "sourceName": "Epoch AI", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "This post is part of Epoch AI’s Gradient Updates newsletter, which shares more opinionated or informal takes on big questions in AI progress. These posts solely represent the views of the authors, and do not necessarily reflect the views of Epoch AI as a whole. OpenAI and Anthropic are growing about as fast, or faster than any company of their size has grown in history. It’s extremely rare for a tech company making more than $1 billion to be growing over 100% per year. 1 In fact, both labs are way above that threshold. OpenAI tripled its revenue run rate (recent revenue extrapolated to an annu"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T1", "firstParty": true, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-056", "material": {"title": "Summary of METR's predeployment evaluation of Claude Opus 5.5", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-22T07:00:00+00:00", "sourceName": "METR", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Note on independence: This evaluation was conducted under an unpaid agreement for AI R&D assessment. 1 We drafted the initial summary, and then Anthropic had the opportunity to review and edit the text. We signed off on this final text from the Claude Opus 5.5 system card . Our preliminary evaluation focused on how Claude Opus 5.5 might impact AI R&D, mainly based on its capabilities on difficult, long-horizon tasks. The main claims we attempt to assess in this report are: (A) would AI R&D at Anthropic now be dramatically accelerated by using Claude Opus 5.5; and (B) was AI R&D at Anthropic al"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T1", "firstParty": true, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "前沿"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-059", "material": {"title": "New Tool Alert: Course Copilot in Canvas", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-03T22:00:36+00:00", "sourceName": "MIT Sloan 教学技术", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Course technology questions often come up while you’re right in the middle of working in Canvas. Now, MIT Sloan teaching teams can get quick, just-in-time answers without leaving their course with Course Copilot , a new AI-powered support tool built directly into Canvas. Setting up and managing course technology can involve a lot of questions: Where do I get started as a new member of the teaching team? How do I create a group assignment in Canvas? How do I set up attendance? Can I try out the classroom technology before my first class? Instead of searching for answers across different resourc"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T1", "firstParty": true, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-060", "material": {"title": "A+ Attendance Update: New Default Check-In Settings", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-08-19T10:23:51+00:00", "sourceName": "MIT Sloan 教学技术", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "A+ Attendance is getting an update for Fall 2026. New default check-in settings will help teaching teams better ensure attendance records reflect who is physically present in the classroom—without requiring any additional setup. We’ve updated the default settings in A+ Attendance to make in-class check-in more secure and help ensure attendance records reflect who is physically present in class. The new defaults use a rolling code that changes every 30 seconds and require students to be on campus and connected to MIT Wi-Fi . The default check-in window for teaching teams that use a static code "}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T1", "firstParty": true, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "holdout", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "reject"}}
{"caseId": "sci-061", "material": {"title": "Simulated Personas, Real Insights: An MIT Sloan Student’s Perspective on AI for User Research", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2025-08-15T22:05:40+00:00", "sourceName": "MIT Sloan 教学技术", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Simulated customer interviews gave me a week’s worth of research insights—but they also forced me to confront AI’s blind spots, including bias, hallucinations and the limits of synthetic data. Simulated interviews may sound artificial, but as a student learning the messy art of customer discovery, they offered something surprisingly real. In my Generative AI for Managers course at MIT Sloan, Professor John Horton asked us to explore user demand using AI personas. As a project for the course, I was designing MindBuddy—a wearable headband that gently nudges you when your focus drifts. For this a"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T1", "firstParty": true, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-062", "material": {"title": "The AI-Ready Canvas Course: Six Steps to Future-Proof Your Teaching", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2025-05-14T16:55:28+00:00", "sourceName": "MIT Sloan 教学技术", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "AI capabilities are rapidly becoming ubiquitous in educational technology. Soon, you’ll be hard-pressed to find learning tools that don’t incorporate some form of AI, no matter where you stand on AI’s role in education. Our team in Sloan Technology Services (STS) has been evaluating AI tools in the educational technology space—ones that make promises of enhancing learning outcomes, improving student engagement, providing personalized feedback, and streamlining administrative tasks. We’ve also built and evaluated our own AI solutions, like the Spring 2025 Course Bot (Beta) experiment . Through "}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T1", "firstParty": true, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-063", "material": {"title": "Spotlight on AI in Education: September 2026", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-29T15:40:06+00:00", "sourceName": "苏塞克斯大学 Learning Matters", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Welcome to September 2026’s Spotlight on AI in Education bulletin. With how fast things are moving, this will help you cut through the noise and catch what’s important. The bulletin highlights on-the-ground practice, institutional perspectives and trends in generative AI use across the sector and beyond. We hope you find this useful. If you have anything you’d like to contribute or see in this bulletin please email EE@sussex.ac.uk AI generated image using CoPilot, 22nd September 2026. HM prompt: a professor explaining AI to a student on a leafy campus in September. We begin 2026/27 with a welc"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T1", "firstParty": true, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-064", "material": {"title": "Developing AI-literate graduates through a values-led, institution-wide approach", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-28T16:01:37+00:00", "sourceName": "苏塞克斯大学 Learning Matters", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "As Head of Educational Enhancement at the University of Sussex, I’ve been involved in many of the conversations that have shaped our response to generative AI. Working with colleagues across the University, students and sector networks, I’ve seen first-hand both the opportunities and challenges that AI presents for learning, teaching and assessment. At the start of a new academic year, it feels like a good moment to reflect on how far we’ve come, what we’ve learned and where we’re heading next. At the University of Sussex, our goal is simple: we want every graduate to be AI-literate, criticall"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T1", "firstParty": true, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "holdout", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
