{"caseId": "sci-079", "material": {"title": "From Points to People: Rethinking Assessment for Life Beyond School", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-30T04:00:00+00:00", "sourceName": "Faculty Focus", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Recently, a faculty member took up residence in Amanda’s office chair and settled in for a chat. As a regular visitor, he first updated her that after four attempts, his daughter had received her driver’s license. As the discussion drifted towards work, he laughingly mentioned that their dean had sent him yet another request for his woefully late annual faculty development report. Their discussion wrapped up with harmless gossip about a professional staff member who had been put on an improvement plan after mismanaging resources. So she was a little surprised a week later when the same faculty"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-080", "material": {"title": "AI in Teaching: Preserving Core Pedagogical Principles", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-28T04:00:00+00:00", "sourceName": "Faculty Focus", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Artificial Intelligence (AI) burst into classrooms without knocking. Before educational institutions could respond, AI was shaping lesson plans, assessment tasks, and student learning. It wasn’t long before AI-generated outputs were observed in student assignments and outcomes. Moreover, the practitioners’ prevailing enthusiasm to embrace AI explore its potential is increasingly evident. AI continues to prove itself a valuable assistant for practitioners in education because of its accessibility, ease of use, and the time and efforts it can save. However, a potential danger remains. If conveni"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "holdout", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-081", "material": {"title": "When the Tool Trips You Up: LiveBinder Glitches, Fixes, and Student Problem Solving", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-25T04:00:00+00:00", "sourceName": "Faculty Focus", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "On paper, LiveBinder seemed ideal for my undergraduate literacy methods course: free, accessible, and built for collaborative content creation. Students could upload documents, annotate websites, and learn from peers, all course goals. I expected the tool to integrate seamlessly. Mostly it did, until it didn’t. Adopting the popular adage of failing forward and falling back on my mother’s wisdom of making lemonade out of lemons, below I describe three problems that emerged, the solutions used, and practical tips other educators can apply immediately. Problem 1: What Email Address Did You Use? W"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "reject"}}
{"caseId": "sci-083", "material": {"title": "The Overhang", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-18T17:54:32+00:00", "sourceName": "One Useful Thing", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "We are still on an exponential curve of AI development. I try to put out a Substack post every couple weeks or so, yet, as the pace speeds up, that sometimes feels too slow. In the weeks since my last post, we had the apparent cracking of one of the most famous problems in math by an AI (accompanied by controversy) and widespread discussions about the risks posed by AI and what to do about it (also accompanied by controversy). I think these concerns, along with a mounting set of other worries, come down to the same problem I have with my posts: how slowly our very human systems and processes w"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-085", "material": {"title": "An opinionated guide to which AI to use to do stuff", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-07-23T18:05:24+00:00", "sourceName": "One Useful Thing", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Every few months, I write a guide for people who want to use AI to do stuff. This time, a lot has changed, in part because what it means to “use AI to do stuff” encompasses so much more “stuff” than it used to. Until recently, using AI meant talking to a model through a chatbot in a constant back-and-forth conversation. Now, it means using an agentic system, where the AI is capable of doing the equivalent of many hours of real human work in one go by combining the brains of an AI model with a set of tools that let it plan and act for you. Basically, an agentic system gives an AI a computer to "}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-087", "material": {"title": "Computer Using AI is Solved", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-06T08:08:10+00:00", "sourceName": "Leon Furze", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Two years ago I wrote an article arguing that Anthropic’s “Computer Use” – then in beta testing – would be the next ChatGPT moment for education. At the time, computer using agents (CUAs) were not common, and Anthropic’s version was strictly limited to research, running only inside a virtual computer. It had restricted access to a few sandboxed applications like the open source LibreOffice, and a limited browser. It’s taken a little while, but we are now seeing the first commercially available, fully functional computer using agents. With the recent releases of Anthropic’s Fable 5.1 and OpenAI"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "holdout", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-088", "material": {"title": "Rogue AI: Should We Be Worried?", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-03T01:45:12+00:00", "sourceName": "Leon Furze", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "In the space of a fortnight, three of the world’s largest AI labs have each admitted that their models broke into somebody else’s computer systems. In the real world, these “rogue” agents are popping up like weeds: an Australian gym-goer was bumped off a waitlist by somebody else’s AI assistant; a government in the Asia-Pacific lost 2,500 personnel records to a swarm of open-source agents; and a podcaster wrote a viral essay about three secret AI civilisations rising and falling inside OpenAI, which racked up the better part of a million views in a weekend. It’s been a strange season… But are "}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "reject"}}
{"caseId": "sci-090", "material": {"title": "What is AI slop?", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-08-30T01:40:11+00:00", "sourceName": "Leon Furze", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "At the risk of sounding like a teenager kicking off a torturous essay, Merriam-Webster defines slop as: digital content of low quality that is produced usually in quantity by means of artificial intelligence https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/slop So far, so concise. But I don’t think this definition really does the term justice. I’ve been writing about slop for a couple of years without really defining it myself in articles like Digital plastic in 2023, The Effort Economy of Slop in March, and AI;DR in July. The closest I came to a definition in any of those posts was lifted from a Bl"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-091", "material": {"title": "What Happens When Grading Costs Almost Nothing?", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-20T12:11:12+00:00", "sourceName": "Rhetorica", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Reihaneh Golpayegani / https://betterimagesofai.org / https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ Jev is a new narrow LLM model that doesn’t generate text—it’s designed to classify, route, score, and choose actions, all based on probabilities. What makes Jev really stand out, and frankly alarming, is how stupefyingly cheap and fast it is to run. Jev is supposedly 200x faster and 400x cheaper than frontier models. You can build and run end-to-end workflows for hundreds of students for literal pennies. The same goes for research tasks. A model like Jev could sweep through the Edtech space, tran"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-093", "material": {"title": "College Students Get Free Premium AI . . . Again", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-08-30T13:13:08+00:00", "sourceName": "Rhetorica", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "A year and a half ago, I wrote about the first wave of free trials AI companies gave away to college students. What started as a one-off marketing campaign has morphed into a perennial ritual timed with the start of the fall semester, aggressively advertising premium AI tools exclusively to college students. Campuses are trying to figure out how to navigate the equity and access issues that ubiquitous free AI and free premium AI plans represent, while most faculty have little understanding or time to explore what advanced AI features can do to their assignments and try to adjust accordingly. O"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "holdout", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-095", "material": {"title": "“Not sufficiently…”: the objections EU evaluators raise most often — and how to answer them before you submit", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-23T22:00:00+00:00", "sourceName": "Research Whisperer", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "In this post, Pascal Kahlem provides advice based on his experience writing and reviewing European Union grant applications. As Research Whisperer editors, we like his approach. In our experience, his advice holds across all research funding schemes, not just European Union funding. I write European research proposals for a living, and I also evaluate them. Sitting on the assessor’s side changed how I read my own drafts. When you score proposals against a rubric, at speed, alongside a dozen other proposals, you stop looking for brilliance and start looking for reasons to be reassured — and rea"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "reject"}}
{"caseId": "sci-099", "material": {"title": "when you hit the page wall", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-14T00:58:58+00:00", "sourceName": "Patter", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Academic writers can spend a lot of time being stuck. Quite often they see stuckness as a thinking problem. So they go back to the sources, re-read the theory, work out what they could say next. And quite often that is exactly what is needed. But there is a different kind of stuck that more reading won’t fix. That’s where the writer knows the argument they want to make but the sentences simply won’t come. It’s as if the document itself has become a wall. It’s as if the physical act of looking at the page has stopped working, and no amount of re-reading will fix it. If that happens to you occas"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "reject"}}
