{"caseId": "sci-106", "material": {"title": "5 Takeaways From Microsoft’s Global AI in Education Survey", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-30T12:37:00+00:00", "sourceName": "OLDaily", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Sarah , Alex Sarlin , Ben Kornell , Jen Lapaz , Edtech Insiders , Sept 30, 2026 This post summarizes a new report from Microsoft on AI and Education based on a survey of a few thousand students from selected countries. The report is behind a spamwall but this direct link to the 33 page PDF might work. The summary follows a standard order of presentation - people are using AI, think learning is important, are concerned about some issues in common, and are cautious about the future. There's a section on internationalization (takeaway 3) that relies more on an earlier Ipsos report . My takeaways "}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-107", "material": {"title": "Pressing Prompts: An OER for AI in Higher Education", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-30T12:37:00+00:00", "sourceName": "OLDaily", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Stefanie Panke , AACE , Sept 30, 2026 According to this article, \" Pressing Prompts is an open collection of teaching activities and resources designed to help higher education instructors engage students in discussions about the social and ethical implications of artificial intelligence.\" It's divided into topics such as 'Can we trust AI?' and 'Is AI biased?' and doesn't try to answer these questions so much as to structure activities and resources to support student discovery around them. Web: [ Direct Link ] [ This Post ]"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
{"caseId": "sci-108", "material": {"title": "FediHome", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-30T12:37:00+00:00", "sourceName": "OLDaily", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Samuel Lison , Sept 30, 2026 I like the ambition of this project though I fear future maintenance because of its dependence on Node. Still, I like how a lot of attention has been paid to ease of installation and startup. \"FediHome is a self-hosted, single-user Fediverse home — blog, photos, video, audio, and a live following feed, all on your own domain via ActivityPub. 1.0 marks the project production-ready: a test suite + CI gate every change, the security-audit findings are resolved, the dependency stack is current (Prisma 7, Next 16, Fedify 2), and a public demo runs the exact code at fedi"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "reject"}}
{"caseId": "sci-111", "material": {"title": "When HQIM Meets the Chatbot", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-21T04:02:25+00:00", "sourceName": "Educating AI", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "There is a Thursday-night version of every teacher. The one who has internalized the curriculum, who knows why the discourse routine in lesson 14 is structured the way it is, who can improvise a scaffold without losing the thread of the unit. That teacher exists. But she exists on a good week, in a well-supported building, after years with the same program. The Thursday-night version is tired. She has a sub plan to write, a student in crisis, and a lesson for tomorrow she hasn’t looked at yet. When a generative AI tool offers her something usable in thirty seconds, she takes it. Not out of neg"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "holdout", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-112", "material": {"title": "AI Swarms, AI Literacy, and the Anxious Classroom", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-15T15:38:52+00:00", "sourceName": "Educating AI", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "A Swarm of Locusts, from Brehms Tierleben, pub. 1860s by German School. Some of your students already know about the Hugging Face swarm. They probably did not read the METR report. In the more likely scenario, they saw a thirty-second Instagram or TikTok clip with a red-tinted thumbnail and a voice saying something like AI formed a secret society, hacked a company, and forged its own records so nobody would find out. By the time the story reaches a fourteen-year-old, it has shed every date and figure and kept only the feeling, which is dread. That feeling is where I want to start, because of i"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "either"}}
{"caseId": "sci-117", "material": {"title": "MIT’s Report on AI in Teaching and Learning", "originalTitle": null, "publishedAt": "2026-09-10T14:17:01+00:00", "sourceName": "Agile Learning", "bodyZh": null, "bodyOriginal": "Cross-posted from my Intentional Teaching newsletter . The Massachusetts Institute of Technology (better known as MIT) released a report last month sharing the findings and recommendations of their Ad Hoc Committee on AI Use in Teaching, Learning, and Research. I’ve read a lot of university reports on AI over the last few years, and most of them are forgettable. This one, however, is fantastic. It’s very MIT, in that it’s grounded in the MIT context, which is not a typical college or university context, but it takes such an informed, realistic, and practical take on AI and higher ed’s response"}, "sourceFacts": {"sourceKind": "rss", "sourceTier": "T2", "firstParty": false, "language": "en"}, "samplingContext": {"benchmarkSplit": "development", "samplingStratum": "实践"}, "gold": {"decision": "select"}}
